## Verification

`mise run check` is the fast quality gate. Use `mise run verify` for product,
integration, browser, artifact, or other claims that need more than formatting,
static analysis, and unit tests. It runs `check` and stack-specific verification.
Custom product routes run only when you name them.

Inspect available routes, choose relevant journeys from code and product context,
and run them explicitly:

```bash
scripts/verify-routes --list
scripts/verify-routes --route create-record
mise run verify -- --route create-record
```

Use repeated `--route` arguments for several journeys and `--all` only for an
intentional broad run. Explain why selected routes cover the change and note
material routes skipped. Keep manual or credentialed checks as explicit operator
evidence; do not mark them as automated passes.

## Task Reference

| Command | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| `mise run setup` | Install dependencies and prepare the environment |
| `mise run fmt` | Auto-format code |
| `mise run lint` | Run lint checks (non-modifying) |
| `mise run typecheck` | Run static type analysis |
| `mise run test` | Run unit tests |
| `mise run build` | Build artifacts |
| `mise run check` | Fast quality gate (fmt + lint + typecheck + test) |
| `mise run verify` | Heavy quality and stack validation; add explicit product route arguments when needed |
{% if project_shape == "apps" %}| `mise run dev -- <module>` | Start local development for a module |{% else %}| `mise run dev` | Start local development |{% endif %}
| `mise run ci` | CI entrypoint (= check) |

